germany has announced a new blueprint  for relations with  asia calling in particular for diversity  in its focus  in the region that's being interpreted  as an effort to lessen  its dependence on china a move that  would  almost certainly have consequences for  german businesses  from its mass internment of uyghurs in  xinjiang  to its crackdown in hong kong and the  sharpened edge of its diplomacy in  recent years  china is politically a growing problem  for germany  economically china is a golden goose  on average german blue chip companies  earn 15  of their revenues from china some go  further  china accounted for more than 40 percent  of turnover for volkswagen in the first  half of 2020  34 for bmw and 22  for adidas and that gives china leverage  in the relationship  the new guidelines from the german  foreign ministry are meant to begin  correcting that imbalance  one key word diversification  spreading out amongst more countries for  strategic and trade partnerships  foreign minister heiko maas told his  chinese counterpart wang gui  this week that his country wanted to be  seen as an equal  we won't allow it that we become the  plaything of a superpower competition  between the united states russia and  china  we want to be in dialogue with everyone  easier said than done many german  businesses are wary of endangering their  relationship with china  well on this let's speak to gabrielle  felbermeier who's president of the keel  institute  for the world economy here in germany  thanks a lot for joining us  so the german government has been pretty  clear in its criticism  of certain chinese policies uh the  treatment of the uyghurs and the  hong kong security law but how much  clout does germany  actually have i mean is it a big enough  trading partner to make a difference  yes germany is a big trading partner of  china it's also a technology partner  and it has grown in its importance  because of course  the united states is decoupling from  china so  indeed germany has clout in this  relationship and  uh is an important factor  in uh pushing china towards more  reciprocity  and towards a more balanced relationship  heiko mars and other european leaders  have talked about  the europe as a whole being a bit more  self-confident  in the face of china what's been lacking  in the european approach there  uh well i think that's very easy it's  unity uh  and you know we all know about the 16  plus one initiative where china  actively tries to drive a wedge  between the european countries uh of  course  germany is much more exposed to the  chinese economy than say france or spain  or italy so there are different  interests within europe that make  unity more difficult but it's important  now with these new guidelines that the  germany  takes the driving seat that it does more  to unite  europe behind a very balanced and  reasonable approach which is now led out  in this new paper  now europe should ultimately be wanting  to compete with the likes of  china and the united states not just  trade with them  what would that look like how can europe  compete with these enormous superpowers  first of all it's important that we  you know get more convinced of our own  strengths  europe is an important uh player in  international trade some say that europe  is the  uh trading superpower we are the largest  trade partner  of many countries and uh it is in  particular this  strategic rivalry between china and the  united states that gives  europe extra clout and so i'm not a  pessimistic  per se what we need to have is a clear  strategy  and what we also need to do is  pursue a balanced approach that is not  not the targeting to restrict trade but  tries to open more trading possibility  with  other partners in asia that's what this  paper is calling for and i think for  that reason it's a very good approach we  need to  push the trade agreements that are being  negotiated with countries like indonesia  or with other asean partners  we need to bring china to the  negotiation table and conclude this  investment agreement that has been  under negotiation for too long now so  there is a lot to be done  and germany can play an important role  here  using those tools of international trade  diplomacy  just very briefly so what are the target  areas of asia you mentioned  uh southeast asia beyond china where's  germany looking  well in this specific paper that's been  published yesterday  that the germany is looking in  particular the asean countries  indonesia being the largest and fastest  growing amongst them  we already have trade agreements for  example with singapore but others  are under negotiation or are in  difficulty for example with thailand or  malaysia  i think this target is exactly right and  the second big power of course in the  area is india  and germany has not yet developed a a  good strategy to deal with that  country that potentially is larger in  terms of population than  uh than china and also has a lot to a  lot ground to grover  to cover in terms of economic  development an  india strategy is as important for this  reason  for this region than a smart way to deal  with china  gabriel fergul meyer from the keel  institute for the world economy thanks  for joining us  you
 

